{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"DIRECTION FROM OPERATOR: do not pin a local update tool. Recording the consequences, and a correction to my own earlier recommendation.\n\nMY RECOMMENDATION WAS WRONG ON THE FACTS. I proposed 'brew upgrade uv' as the minimal unblock. That cannot work: required-version is '==0.12.7', an EXACT pin, and Homebrew has moved PAST it to 0.12.9 (2026-09-01). Verified empirically against a scratch pyproject carrying the same pin:\n  /opt/homebrew/bin/uv 0.12.9  -> error: Required uv version `==0.12.7` does not match the running version `0.12.9`\n  ~/.local/bin/uv     0.12.7  -> Resolved 1 package\nUpgrading Homebrew only changes which number appears in the error. The scheduled 00:32 run confirms it independently: it failed with 0.12.9 and pushed the streak to 120. I should have checked the pin's comparison operator before recommending an upgrade.\n\nTHIS IS THE FEBD FAILURE, VERSION FOR VERSION. scripts/check_uv_pin.py's docstring describes the outage as: 'every build died with Required uv version ==0.12.7 does not match the running version 0.12.9 the moment :latest moved -- a time bomb armed by an upstream release, with no change to this repository.' The version in that sentence is 0.12.9, which is what Homebrew now ships. So this host's updater is the SAME time bomb with a different fuse: not a floating Docker tag, but a package manager that upgraded past the pin. Four consumers of one exact pin, three of which were never brought along.\n\nRELAXING THE PIN IS NOT AVAILABLE. check_uv_pin.py deliberately fails the build when the pin is 'loosened to a range' (its point 2) or overridden per call site (point 3), because an exact pin is the property under test. So the fix cannot be a wider constraint in pyproject.toml.\n\nWHAT THE DIRECTION MEANS FOR AC2. The updater is a local dev-tracking tool, not CI; it gains no reproducibility from being locked to any particular uv binary, and the current hardcode (UV_BIN default /opt/homebrew/bin/uv at rebar-dev-update.sh:18) is itself a local pin -- the one that broke. So AC2 stands as written and should be read as explicitly EXCLUDING a new hardcoded path: the updater must DERIVE the uv it needs from the CANDIDATE COMMIT's own required-version at sync time, since that value changes as main moves, and fail loudly naming required version, found version, and paths inspected when none satisfies it. Substituting one absolute path for another (either direction) is rejected -- it re-inverts the moment the other side updates first, which is exactly how this stall was born.\n\nAC3 RESCOPED per operator decision: do NOT pursue the cross-account SNS authorization. The alert sink stays as-is. AC4 (a LOCAL detector that surfaces a stalled updater or an over-stale build without depending on the remote sink) absorbs the detection requirement and is the priority half -- it is the thing that would actually have caught 120 silent failures.\n\nIMMEDIATE STATE: no persistent host change made. No plist edit, no REBAR_DEV_UV_BIN pin. Homebrew's uv did move 0.12.6 -> 0.12.9 during investigation (a relink surfaced by 'brew upgrade'; both versions fail the pin identically, so no behavior changed). The global rebar remains at cdfd7d94d and stale until AC2 lands or an operator runs a one-shot."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788489316259929001,"uuid":"a31c8d9f-cdca-4fbd-bae3-ae1a1ee8532f"}